+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for lawyers in 2026?
+The strongest open source options in this list are [OpenContracts](https://github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts) for legal-specific document work, [LexNLP](https://github.com/LexPredict/lexpredict-lexnlp) for legal text extraction, [OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF) for scanned PDFs, and [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm) or [PrivateGPT](https://github.com/zylon-ai/private-gpt) for private document Q&A.
+
+### Are there any truly free AI contract review tools for lawyers on GitHub?
+Yes, but most are infrastructure rather than polished consumer products. [OpenContracts](https://github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts), [ContraxSuite](https://github.com/LexPredict/lexpredict-contraxsuite), and Accord Project repositories are the closest open source contract-focused options. The gap is that many commercial contract review tools still do not have comparable open GitHub repos.
+
+### Which self-hosted AI tools are best for confidential legal documents?
+[PrivateGPT](https://github.com/zylon-ai/private-gpt), [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm), [Open WebUI](https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui), [Haystack](https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack), and [LlamaIndex](https://github.com/run-llama/llama_index) are the best starting points here. Pair them with local OCR and document parsing tools such as OCRmyPDF, Docling, or Unstructured.
+
+### What open source AI tools help lawyers with scanned PDFs and court filings?
+[OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF), [Tesseract](https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract), [pdfplumber](https://github.com/jsvine/pdfplumber), [MinerU](https://github.com/opendatalab/MinerU), and [Layout Parser](https://github.com/Layout-Parser/layout-parser) are the most useful open source building blocks for scanned and layout-heavy legal documents.
+
+### Is there an open source alternative to proprietary legal research AI?
+Partially. Public legal research stacks exist, but they are less polished than proprietary offerings. [OLDP](https://github.com/openlegaldata/oldp), [Legal Reference Extraction](https://github.com/openlegaldata/legal-reference-extraction), and benchmark or dataset projects such as [LexGLUE](https://github.com/coastalcph/lex-glue) are useful, but this remains a real market gap.
+
+### Are there open source AI tools for law firms that include billing and case management?
+Not many credible GitHub-native options surfaced during research. This is an honest gap in open source legal tech: AI-assisted document analysis and retrieval are much stronger than full practice management, timekeeping, or legal billing platforms.
+
